.nf
.sp 0.6i
.ft B
Shoe-pair discrepancy - 1/5
.ft R
.sp 0.3i
\&A UNIFORM DISCREPANCY BOUND FOR ALTERNATING SHOE PAIRS MathDB 324571 - implementation
\&report, 8 November 2026 UTC
.sp 0.5
\&1. Statement and exact source experiment For every discrete probability law p, the total
\&variation distance between the conditional matching-pair color X and the first chronological
\&left-right matching color Y satisfies D(p,p) <= 255/256. Consequently sup_p D(p,p) <=
\&255/256 < 1. This addresses the strict bound, not the optimal-constant conjecture.
.sp 0.5
\&The copied full MathDB literal asks sup_p D(p,p)<1 without a support-size restriction.
\&Arratia and DeSalvo, On the Random Sampling of Pairs, with Pedestrian Examples,
\&arXiv:1211.6486v2 (2013), Sections 2, 4, 9.1-9.2, Conjecture 4 equation (30), specifies the
\&model. The final version, Advances in Applied Probability 47 (2015), 292-305, DOI
\&10.1239/aap/1427814592, Sections 9.1-9.2, Conjecture 4 equation (19), retains it. Their
\&Theorem 3 gives a limiting-family formula, not a universal bound. Original and final copied
\&bodies supply the actual definitions.
.sp 0.5
\&Colors are numbered by Nat in the source, with finite alphabets padded by zero. Independent
\&colors arrive as L1,R1,L2,R2,... . A match requires one shoe of each side. If the first
\&match occurs on a new arrival, its color is the new shoe's color; multiple matching partners
\&cannot create a color tie. Simultaneous-pair and independently randomized-side rules are
\&different experiments and are not used.
.sp 0.5
\&Put s=sum_i p_i^2. Then 0<s<=1, p_i<=sqrt(s), and x_i=P(X=i)=p_i^2/s. With iid infinite
\&coordinates, no match through 2N arrivals implies all N aligned left-right pairs are
\&unequal. Thus P(T>2N)<=(1-s)^N. This tends to zero, proving almost-sure termination.
\&Arbitrary fallback colors on the null nontermination event do not change the law.
.sp 0.5
\&The formal model uses PMF, Measure.infinitePi, a prefix HasMatch predicate, Nat.find for the
\&least matching prefix, and measurable winner pushforwards. ConditionalColorMeasure is the
\&normalized restriction of the genuine iid space to equality of its first two coordinates,
\&then projection to the first color. No desired law relation or bound is an assumption.
.sp 0.5
.br
.bp
.sp 0.6i
.ft B
Shoe-pair discrepancy - 2/5
.ft R
.sp 0.3i
\&2. Ordinary proof of the uniform constant Write y_i=P(Y=i). We prove y_i>=x_i/256 pointwise.
\&If s>=1/64, L1=R1=i forces Y=i, so y_i>=p_i^2=s*x_i>=x_i/256. This includes point masses and
\&the threshold equality.
.sp 0.5
\&If 0<s<1/64, set K=floor(1/(4sqrt(s))). Since the quantity floored exceeds 2,
\&K>=1/(8sqrt(s)), K<=1/(4sqrt(s)), K^2>=1/(64s), and K^2*s<=1/16. A zero coordinate is
\&immediate. For a positive coordinate, p_i<=sqrt(s)<1/8, so 1-p_i>7/8 and K*p_i<=1/4.
.sp 0.5
\&For a,b among the first K positions on each side, let E_ab specify that exactly L_a and R_b
\&have color i within this block. Its probability is p_i^2*(1-p_i)^(2K-2). These K^2 events
\&are disjoint. Let B_ab be the event of another cross-side match, avoiding the two designated
\&positions. Conditional on E_ab, the remaining coordinates are independent with masses
\&p_j/(1-p_i) for j != i. A competing pair has equality probability at most s/(1-p_i)^2. There
\&are (K-1)^2 such pairs. The union bound therefore gives P(B_ab|E_ab)<=4/49<1/2.
.sp 0.5
\&An unconditional implementation avoids assuming that conditional independence bridge. For
\&each competing position pair c,d and color j!=i, the intersection with E_ab is a finite
\&cylinder: four distinct designated coordinates contribute p_i^2*p_j^2 and the other 2K-4
\&coordinates contribute (1-p_i)^(2K-4). Countable disjoint summation over j bounds this by
\&p_i^2*s*(1-p_i)^(2K-4). The finite union over c,d bounds all competitors. Combining this
\&with the exact E_ab mass yields the same lower bound P(E_ab minus
\&B_ab)>=(1/2)*p_i^2*(1-p_i)^(2K-2). This is the actual ShoeCollision and ShoeMinorization
\&route; no independence of competing-pair events is asserted.
.sp 0.5
\&On F_ab=E_ab minus B_ab, i is the only common color on the two sides. The later designated
\&arrival is the chronological first match, in either arrival order. Thus F_ab implies Y=i.
\&Bernoulli's inequality gives (1-p_i)^(2K-2)>=1-2K*p_i>=1/2. Summing disjoint F_ab gives
\&y_i>=(K^2/4)*p_i^2>=p_i^2/(256s)=x_i/256.
.sp 0.5
\&The countable series sum_i |x_i-y_i| converges, being bounded termwise by x_i+y_i. Hence
\&dTV(X,Y)=1-sum_i min(x_i,y_i)<=255/256. The subset-supremum convention agrees with half the
\&l1 sum: the positive-difference subset attains it, and every subset is bounded by that
\&value. The same constant for every p bounds the supremum strictly below 1.
.sp 0.5
.br
.bp
.sp 0.6i
.ft B
Shoe-pair discrepancy - 3/5
.ft R
.sp 0.3i
\&3. Arbitrary countable colors and finite zero extension Let A be any countable discrete
\&measurable alphabet and p a PMF on A. Countability supplies an injective code e:A->Nat; no
\&ordering of colors or positive-support assumption is imposed. Push p forward as p.map e.
\&Coordinatewise relabeling pushes the actual infinite iid product measure to the Nat product
\&measure. This follows from the product-measure mapping theorem and the PMF-to-measure
\&mapping identity.
.sp 0.5
\&Injectivity preserves HasMatch at each prefix, hence FirstMatch, termination, and the least
\&prefix length. On terminating paths the winner is mapped by e. With the fallback mapped as
\&well, the explicitly defined colorWinner commutes pathwise with coding even on
\&nonterminating paths. Its measurability is proved by countable winner-fiber decomposition.
\&The resulting winner pushforward measure is exactly the Nat sourceWinner measure after
\&coding.
.sp 0.5
\&For the conditional experiment, equality of the first two coordinates is preserved by
\&injectivity. Its probability becomes the Nat collisionMass. Mapping the normalized
\&restriction and first-coordinate projection gives exactly conditionalColorMeasure of p.map
\&e. This proves transport of the actual conditional law rather than postulating a relation
\&between arbitrary PMFs. Separate normalization certificates show the conditional and winner
\&measures are probability measures and that their toPMF pushforwards are exactly sourceX and
\&sourceY.
.sp 0.5
\&For any measures mu,nu on the discrete countable alphabet, the range of absolute subset-
\&probability differences agrees with that of their injective pushforwards. A source subset is
\&represented by its image; a Nat subset pulls back. This proves equality of the TV suprema.
\&The source-defined colorDiscrepancy therefore equals sourceDiscrepancy(p.map e,e(c)). The
\&existing Nat bound now proves countable_color_discrepancy_bound and
\&countable_color_strict_universal_supremum; no duplicate elementary inequality proof is
\&introduced.
.sp 0.5
\&Finite alphabets use Fin.val. The iid law, chronological winner, conditional law and
\&discrepancy are preserved. Every Nat index outside the finite range has PMF mass exactly
\&zero. Zero coordinates, finite support, singleton laws and countably infinite support are
\&permitted. The empty alphabet admits no PMF or fallback; no probability law is excluded by
\&requiring a fallback element. Generic almost-sure termination follows by pulling back the
\&Nat null event, and fallback independence follows on this full-measure event.
.sp 0.5
\&Formal map: ShoeRelabel handles chronology; ShoeColorMeasure handles iid coding;
\&ShoeColorWinner handles measurable winner transport; ShoeColorConditional handles normalized
\&conditioning; ShoeColorEndpoint handles TV and the universal supremum;
\&ShoeColorNormalization and ShoeColorPMF provide probability and PMF certificates. The
\&original Nat core is unchanged.
.sp 0.5
.br
.bp
.sp 0.6i
.ft B
Shoe-pair discrepancy - 4/5
.ft R
.sp 0.3i
\&4. Kernel checks, body audit and extracted replay Pinned Lean is 4.34.1; mathlib revision is
\&d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612. The actual Lean version, binary hashes and mathlib
\&HEAD metadata were checked through the lane runner. Strict builds use -j1 -M3500
\&-DwarningAsError=true. All 20 baseline sources were cold-compiled from an empty build before
\&the first new proof. All 28 final proof/control/named-audit modules passed strict
\&compilation and leanchecker.
.sp 0.5
\&Classification is explicit in results/PRODUCTION-PLAN.json: 23 core modules, three controls,
\&two named-axiom audit modules. ShoeReachableAudit is a tool, never imported into a
\&mathematical endpoint. These are modules supporting one candidate, not separate credited
\&results.
.sp 0.5
\&Three inherited deep-audit attempts failed with exit -6 while serializing the whole imported
\&environment. Their logs and receipts remain immutable inputs. The repaired tool first
\&enumerates actual production-module origins, including private/generated declarations, then
\&traverses the shared reachable type/body graph with a visited set. Each node records origin,
\&dependencies, body presence, axiom status and unsafe flag once. Unrelated imported
\&declarations are not dumped. Documentary Python only merges already exported metadata and
\&checks consistency and edge completeness; it performs no mathematics.
.sp 0.5
\&The expanded audit completed with real exit 0 under unchanged caps. It enumerates 259
\&production-origin declarations and 29,367 reachable nodes. The Nat supremum closure has
\&29,263 nodes; the new countable supremum closure has 29,308. Exact closures for the
\&minorization, TV identity, sourceY identity, conditional law, normalization and transport
\&endpoints are saved. The entire graph and every listed endpoint closure have only propext,
\&Classical.choice and Quot.sound as axioms, with no unsafe flags. Named print-axioms output
\&alone is not this audit.
.sp 0.5
\&The fresh extracted replay began with an empty replay/build. All 28 sources were extracted
\&as text; no oleans were copied. Its Lean path contains replay/build and pinned upstream
\&libraries only. Each module passed strict compilation followed by leanchecker sequentially.
\&Source and extracted-source hashes were compared after checker execution, and fresh olean
\&hashes recorded in results/REPLAY.json.
.sp 0.5
\&Positive controls include singleton instantiation, unused finite colors of mass zero, finite
\&discrepancy invariance and distinct-coordinate chronology. Intentional semantic-mutation
\&failures are retained separately and never imported by proofs. Logs preserve actual exits.
\&Further isolated-control checks and the final artifact inventory are recorded alongside this
\&report.
.sp 0.5
.br
.bp
.sp 0.6i
.ft B
Shoe-pair discrepancy - 5/5
.ft R
.sp 0.3i
\&5. Scientific limits, process history and disposition This report establishes a checked
\&candidate bound, not an optimal constant, acceptance, credit, or a worldwide priority claim.
\&The campaign remains 55/60 new results beyond 39 prior results. This lane writes no ledger
\&and assigns itself credit zero. Independent final source-to-Lean, proof, deep-body and
\&manuscript review and root acceptance are separate obligations.
.sp 0.5
\&The copied independent ordinary review found no mathematical gap in the full-scope block
\&proof. Its bounded primary comparison distinguishes the source's limiting-family theorem and
\&Bhattacharya's random-side, diffuse-array collision-time results from deterministic
\&alternating marked-color laws. The Galbraith-Holmes 2012 body returned 404 and remains
\&unread. No alternate delivery was fetched here. Registry-head and indexed-search nonmatches
\&do not clear all proof bodies. The MathDB challenge delivery and all inherited access
\&denials remain binding. No network work occurred in this continuation.
.sp 0.5
\&Historical deviations are preserved in inputs/ROOT-INTEGRATION.json and the previous
\&checkpoint: uncapped early displays, alternate runpy CLI displays, a brief replay/control
\&overlap, truncated broad dumps, a checker following a failed compilation, omitted tool-
\&session display, and failed PDF/render attempts. The historical claim that no paper/endpoint
\&was completed describes an earlier stage; the inherited later three-page PDF and Nat
\&endpoint were completed. Neither fact erases the failures. The updated report supersedes the
\&inherited report's unpaid transport/audit statements only to the extent paid by actual
\&current receipts.
.sp 0.5
\&This continuation used the new own-cwd runner and the explicit prospective front-capped
\&display_log exception. Bulk copied receipt metadata were machine-read and retained; not
\&every bulk receipt entry was personally displayed. Relevant source definitions, literal
\&statement, ordinary proof and substantive checkpoint decisions were displayed. All
\&substantive reads, API inspections, compiler and audit jobs used the immutable lane runner.
\&The first new proof write was the chronology relabel certificate, checked before broader API
\&inspection. Baseline proof sources and frozen helpers were not edited.
.sp 0.5
\&New failed strict attempts remain in receipts, including deprecated-name warnings promoted
\&to errors, API argument mistakes and set-expression rewrite diagnostics. Lean's failed
\&elaborations may print synthetic placeholders in diagnostics; no such placeholder is present
\&in an accepted source or successful replay. No sorry, added axiom, unsafe declaration,
\&native_decide, solver, numerical experiment, network, git, installation, child agent or
\&protected-peer inspection was used to discharge a proof.
.sp 0.5
\&Caps are per runner group: two CPUs, CPU120, AS12GiB, sampled RSS6GiB guard, development
\&guard130 and outer wall140. Sampling is not a perfect cgroup bound. Actual receipt exits and
\&empty remaining-group lists, rather than blanket process guarantees, are the evidence. Final
\&frozen verification, hashes, source map, inventory and precise unpaid review gates accompany
\&the handback.
.sp 0.5
.br
